**2026-08-21: 131,072-token / fp8-KV / 4-session shape, WITH PREFIX CACHING.** Two
changes landed on top of the 2026-08-20 record below, and three of its figures moved:

| | 2026-08-20 | now |
|---|---|---|
| `CHAT_MAX_NUM_SEQS` | 5 | **4** |
| prefix caching | off (believed impossible) | **on, both 27B chat arms** (`align` mode) |
| KV pool | 714,116 tokens | **690,312** (align costs +3.45% per sequence) |
| boot concurrency | 5.45x | **5.27x** (gate is ≥ 4, passes) |
| preemptions | "zero across every measurement" | **not true at full context** — see below |

`--max-num-seqs` is 4 because 5 never held five sequences resident and cost 45% of aggregate
throughput. And "zero preemptions" no longer holds. Three runs of 4 concurrent ~117.5-117.9k-token
distinct prompts with 8,000 forced output tokens each: **cold 0.989 peak / +1 preemption**, **cold
1.000 peak (pool exhausted) / +2**, and **warm at 88.1% reuse, 0.755 peak / zero**. Cold 4-way
preempts reproducibly; prefix caching buys KV headroom, not just latency. No run starved anything
(all four within 1.3 s), which is the difference from 5.

**What is established, and what is NOT.** One shared 474-block pool (473 usable);
`kv_cache_usage_perc = ((num_gpu_blocks-1) - free_blocks)/(num_gpu_blocks-1)`, i.e. **block
occupancy, not attention tokens**. The 48 `linear_attention` + 16 `full_attention` layers group into
1 attention + 3 mamba groups, and the boot line sums all four at `84 + 3x2 = 90` blocks/request —
`474/90 = 5.27x`, and `int(5.2667 x 131,072) = 690,312`, reproducing both engine numbers exactly.
That is the *intended timely-reclamation residency*, NOT an enforced ceiling, so "4 x 90 = 360 fits"
does **not** on its own explain away the measured preemptions.

Established: a preemption is a **genuine instantaneous shortage of allocatable blocks** —
`allocate_slots` returns `None` when required > available and the scheduler then preempts; and
eviction reclaims only `ref_cnt == 0` blocks, because for `ref_cnt > 0` it evicts hash metadata and
never pool ownership, which makes pinned blocks structurally non-evictable. Also established: this is
not driven by output length — 8,000 → 2,000 forced output tokens left the peak at 1.000 and still
preempted once.

**NOT established — do not assert a mechanism.** *Why* the pool runs short is open. Two candidate
stories are each partly refuted: "dense retention allocates ~225 blocks/request" is wrong (align
null-pads and allocates at most ONE physical mamba block per call), and the `--async-scheduling`
reclaim-lag story does not apply (that flag is **not** enabled here — verified against the container
command, rendered compose, env and boot log). See `## NEXT SESSION`.

**`--max-num-seqs` stays 4.** It is not the lever: mamba pages are taken per running request from the
shared pool, whose sizing has no `max_num_seqs` term, so N buys admission control rather than
capacity. *An earlier version of this section claimed 120 blocks/request were required and that N=3
was the ceiling — that was wrong, and is corrected here.*

`README.md` "The shipped shape" and `.env` carry the full tables and the source references; the probe
is `artifacts/probes/probe_prefix_saturation.py`, logs under `results/prefix-saturation/`.

