**DRIVER / KERNEL SUBSTRATE CHANGE — RESOLVED AND RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-20.** An unattended upgrade
at 06:40 replaced the NVIDIA driver userspace (580.159.03 → 580.173.02) while the old kernel module
was still loaded, so `nvidia-smi` failed with `Driver/library version mismatch` and no container
could reach the GPU. The reboot is done: **driver 580.173.02 / kernel 7.0.0-29-generic**, userspace
and module agree, `/var/run/reboot-required` clear. A later manual `apt upgrade -y` at 15:37 found
the NVIDIA userspace already at 580.173.02 and introduced no new mismatch.

The recurrence guard is **in place and verified on the host**: the blacklist lives in the drop-in
`/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52-nvidia-no-unattended` (`"nvidia-";` / `"libnvidia-";`), not in
`50unattended-upgrades` — grep the drop-in, not the base file, when checking it. `apt-mark showhold`
is still empty by design; the blacklist, not a hold, is the mechanism.

Because a driver *and* kernel change is a substrate change, the KV constants were re-read from the
boot log rather than trusted. **They came back identical to the pre-change shipped shape** — 22.7 GiB
/ 714,116 tokens / **5.45x** — so the substrate change moved nothing and every figure below still
stands.

**A RESTART TRAP THAT COST A BOOT, WRITE THIS ONE DOWN.** After the reboot the stack was brought up
with all engines at once instead of staged. chat-fp8 spends ~128 s in `torch.compile` + FlashInfer
autotuning between loading weights and sizing its KV pool, and the pooling engines loaded their
weights *inside* that window (chat weights 13:40:31, embedding 13:40:50, reranker 13:41:37, chat KV
sized 13:42:35). vLLM charged their ~15.5 GiB to chat's own budget — `consumed memory (weights +
non-torch)` read **43.95 GiB against real weights of 28.43 GiB** — and chat got **7.49 GiB /
235,025 tokens / 1.79x**, silently failing the `>= 5` gate while all four containers reported
`healthy` and every model answered correctly. Recreating **chat-fp8 alone**, with the pooling pair
already resident and settled, restored 28.74 GiB consumed / 22.7 GiB KV / 714,116 tokens / 5.45x
with no config change.

The lesson is that container health does NOT prove the shape: an engine that under-profiled its KV
reports `healthy` and returns correct answers on every functional probe (chat, embedding dims,
rerank ordering all passed by hand at 1.79x) while serving a third of the intended concurrency.
`smoke.sh` was NOT run in the degraded state, so whether it would have caught this is untested —
it asserts behaviour, not the KV shape, so assume it would have passed. **Always re-check
`Maximum concurrency for 131,072` after any restart**, and stage the boot — biggest allocator last.

