other two marginally `fp8`, and a paired test over all 2,760 items bounding the gap at **at most 1.7
points**, most likely 0.7) while `nvfp4` is measurably the cheaper engine (**629 vs 499 tok/s**, 1.26x,
on the `off` cells at equal emitted volume, and 12.7% more KV at the same fraction). Effort moves the model far
more than the checkpoint does, and `gpqa_diamond` gets *worse* at `xhigh` because 17–18% of its items
exhaust the 32,768-token budget.

### Two harness faults were emptying `content`, and they dominate at OPPOSITE effort levels

This is the single most important thing on this page. Both are fixed in `evals/lmeval_think.py`;
removing either half re-opens one of them. Measured head-to-head on identical documents:

| effort | population | stop **sent** | stop **withheld** |
|---|---|---|---|
| `low` | 82 prompts | 54/82 empty | 56/82 empty — **no effect** |
| `xhigh` | 70 documents | 46/70 empty (66%) | **3/70 empty (4%)** |

- **At `low`: the model never emits `</think>`.** It finishes reasoning, states the answer *inside*
  the reasoning block, then emits EOS. The `qwen3` parser cannot close the segment, so `content` is
  `null` and the finished answer sits in `reasoning_content` — which upstream lm_eval never reads
  (`openai_completions.py` takes `message["content"]` and nothing else). Verified by replaying the 82
  failures: `finish_reason` `stop` on every one, `stop_reason` `None`, `completion_tokens` 412–2589
  against a 32768 budget, reasoning ending cleanly on a stated answer, and **24/24** carrying an
  extractable `answer is (X)` of which **20/24 correct**. Fixed by falling back to `reasoning_content`.
- **At `xhigh`: vLLM matches client stop strings inside the reasoning segment** ([vLLM #52393](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/52393),
  open, PR #52486 unmerged, reported against 0.21.1/0.25.2.dev and reproduced here on **0.27.1**).
  `mmlu_pro` sends `stop: ["Question:"]` and `gsm8k` sends it too; a long CoT restates it and
  generation dies mid-`<think>`. Fixed by withholding `stop` and applying the task's `until` locally.

Why the split: reasoning **length**. ~500 completion tokens at `low` against a measured ~9,490-token
mean at `xhigh` is ~19× more text in which to type `Question:`. A conclusion drawn at one effort level
does not transfer — the low-effort A/B alone said #52393 was irrelevant, and that was wrong for xhigh.

Result on the two worst cells, re-run with the fixed harness at identical sampler and budget:

| cell | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| `fp8` mmlu_pro `low` | 53.3, 82/210 empty | **77.6 ±2.8**, 0 empty, 72 recovered, 0 truncated |
| `fp8` mmlu_pro `xhigh` | 30.0, 132/210 empty | **78.6 ±5.0**, 0 empty, 3 recovered, 0 truncated |

### `recovered_from_reasoning` vs `truncated_unscored` — never conflate them

Every cell records both, and the distinction is deliberate:

- **`recovered_from_reasoning`** — `content` empty, `finish_reason == "stop"`. A *finished* answer that
  the parser stranded. Read from `reasoning_content` and scored. This is legitimate for this stack:
  `README.md` already tells consumers they "must read `reasoning_content` **or** `reasoning`".
- **`truncated_unscored`** — `content` empty, `finish_reason` anything else. The 32768 budget ran out
  mid-thought, so the reasoning is *unfinished* and may hold a tentative answer the model was still
  arguing with. **Deliberately left unscored.** Recovering these would inflate the score, destroy the
  one measurement the grid makes about a 32768-token deployment, and erase the evidence — a recovered
  item is no longer empty and so vanishes from `null_content` and from the collector's dagger.

Measured across the finished grid: `truncated_unscored` is 0 at every `off` cell, 0–2 at `low` and
`medium`, and **22 (fp8) / 23 (nvfp4) at `xhigh`**, essentially all of it in `gpqa_diamond` — 17–18%
of that task's items at that effort. The xhigh probe had measured 0 of 70 and was simply too small to
see it. This is real deployment behaviour at a 32,768-token budget, not a bug to fix: at `xhigh` on
hard science questions this model runs out of room to think roughly one time in six.

**Optional speedup, not applied.** `max-num-seqs 20` is sized so *every* sequence could reach full
context without preemption. Measured reality is a ~9,500-token mean and KV at ~11%, so sizing for p95
(~20k) would allow ~43 sequences and roughly halve wall-clock, at the cost of possible preemption on
an unlucky batch — visible immediately in `vllm:num_preemptions_total`. It was not safe to change mid-grid, and the grid is
now finished, so this is once again available: `max-num-seqs` is part of the cell fingerprint (see
Resume), so changing it means the next grid refuses to reuse any existing cell and every cell is
re-measured. Archive `results/eval/` first, and change it only between whole runs.

</details>

