   `--kv-cache-dtype` is now **per-arm** (`KV_CACHE_DTYPE_FP8` / `_NVFP4`, falling back to the shared
   `KV_CACHE_DTYPE`). It had to be: both arms read one variable, so shipping `fp8` would have replaced
   the NVFP4 arm's *calibrated static* scales with uncalibrated dynamic ones at identical memory cost
   — a pure downgrade on the inactive arm. `eval-grid.sh` unsets both per-arm variables
   unconditionally, so its A/B still forces one identical dtype on both arms.

   **Two measured results that were not predicted, both in `README.md`:**
   - **fp8 KV moves cost from decode to prefill.** Prefill at 130k went 34.98 → 38.80 s (**+10.9%**),
     single-stream decode 37.4 → 41.8 tok/s (**+11.8%**). Assuming no prefix caching — true when this
     was measured, **retracted 2026-08-20**, see below — the prefill is paid
     every turn, so the crossover is **1,355 output tokens** at a 130k context: below it this shape is
     *slower* per turn (+8.1% at 200 output tokens), above it faster. The measured mean for
     `chat-model-medium` is 1,792 tokens/request, just above — so a wash on average (−1.5%) and a real
     loss on short deep-context tool calls. If your turns are mostly short, unset `KV_CACHE_DTYPE_FP8`.
   - **`request_timeout: 2700` survives, and the extrapolation that said it would not was wrong.**
     Projecting the saturated ITL linearly from 4 streams gave 2,286 s at a thin 1.18x. **Measured** at
     5 streams / 98,305 tokens / 3,072-token budget with all five streams overlapping: ITL 0.0513 s
     over 13,710 tokens = 19.5 tok/s per stream, worst TTFT 126.2 s → **1,807 s, i.e. 1.49x** — better
     than the previous shape's 1.47x, because fp8 KV's cheaper reads offset the extra stream. Decode
     contention is strongly sublinear; **do not extrapolate it**, measure with
     `./scripts/measure-ttft.py --streams N`. The one regression: worst-case TTFT for an admitted
     request went 93.8 → 126.2 s (five serialized prefills, each ~10% slower).

   Raw measurements: `results/ttft/ttft-kvfp8.json` (sweep + 5-way at 130k) and
   `results/ttft/ttft-kvfp8-deep.json` (the 5-way 98k leg the timeout is derived from).

3. **Stop unattended-upgrades from swapping the GPU driver mid-run.** On 2026-08-20 06:40 an
   automatic security update replaced the NVIDIA driver userspace (580.159.03 → 580.173.02) while the
   old kernel module was loaded, so `nvidia-smi` failed with `Driver/library version mismatch` and no
   container could reach the GPU until a reboot. It hit an idle box; four hours into a 6.5-hour grid
   it would have destroyed the run, and a driver change silently alters numerics. `apt-mark showhold`
   is empty and `APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade` is `1`, with the driver served from
   `noble-security/restricted`, so **it will recur**. Targeted fix in
   `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades`:

   Put it in a **drop-in**, `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52-nvidia-no-unattended`, not in
   `50unattended-upgrades` — and note **two corrections to the version this section used to give**,
   both established by reading the shipped code rather than by running it:

   ```
   Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
           "nvidia-";
           "libnvidia-";
   };
   ```

   - **No leading `^`.** `is_pkgname_in_blacklist` matches with `re.match`
     (`/usr/bin/unattended-upgrade:1008`), which is already anchored, and
     `pinning_from_regex_list` builds the apt pin as `'/^' + regex + '/'` (`:258`) — so `"^nvidia-"`
     compiles to `/^^nvidia-/`. GNU ERE tolerates the doubled anchor (tested: still matches), so the
     old form worked, but it leans on behaviour POSIX leaves undefined for no benefit. The stock
     file's own example (`"linux-"`, documented as "matches all packages starting with linux-") is
     the convention.
   - **A drop-in, and NOT because `50unattended-upgrades` is a dpkg conffile — it is not.**
     `dpkg -S` finds no owner for it and it is absent from the package's conffile list. It is
     **ucf-managed**: the postinst runs `ucf --three-way --debconf-ok /usr/share/unattended-upgrades/50unattended-upgrades /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades`.
     The `/etc` copy is currently byte-identical to that template, so a local edit means an
     interactive three-way merge prompt the next time the template changes — which on an unattended
     run stalls or silently resolves to a default, potentially dropping the policy that protects the
     GPU. APT **appends** list options across files in lexical order (verified empirically with
     `apt-config dump` over a throwaway parts directory), so a `52-` file merges rather than replaces.

   Mechanism, so nobody wonders whether this only warns: blacklist entries become apt pins at
   `NEVER_PIN = -32768` (`:118`, `:291`), applied **inside the unattended-upgrades run only**. Manual
   `apt install` / `apt upgrade` is unaffected — which is the reason to prefer this over
   `apt-mark hold`, alongside hold needing exact names (22 installed packages here) that change with
   every driver series.

   Deliberately **not** kernel packages: a new kernel does not break the running one, and
   `nvidia-kernel-source-580-open` is installed so DKMS builds the module for it at install time. The
   trade is that driver CVEs then need applying by hand — schedule them with a reboot and re-read the
   substrate fingerprint afterwards. Note `nvidia-container-toolkit` also matches `nvidia-`, so it is
   frozen from unattended runs too; that is consistent with the deferred-upgrades note below, which
   already wanted it taken deliberately.

   **APPLIED 2026-08-20 and verified.** `apt-config dump Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist`
   returns both patterns and `unattended-upgrade --dry-run --debug` reports
   `Blacklist iniziale: nvidia- libnvidia-`. (Do not confuse that with the separate
   `Packages blacklist due to conffile prompts: []` line, which is a different list.) Replaying the
   shipped `re.match` over the installed package set: **22 packages** captured, and `libcurl4t64`,
   `linux-image-7.0.0-29-generic` and `docker-compose-plugin` correctly not captured.

   **WHY IT WAS NEEDED, which only became clear on checking the origins.** `Allowed-Origins` on this
   host is `${distro_id}:${distro_codename}` and `-security` **only** — `-updates` is commented out.
   The driver packages sit in **both** `noble-updates/restricted` *and*
   `noble-security/restricted`, and the latter is allowed. That is the exact path the 06:40 upgrade
   took, and without this drop-in the next driver security update takes it again.

   **And a cost this section previously overstated: there is none.** An earlier note said the
   blacklist also freezes `nvidia-container-toolkit` (pending `1.20.0~rc.1-1` → `1.20.0-1`) as an
   accepted side effect. It does match `nvidia-`, but those four packages come from NVIDIA's own
   repository, whose origin is **not** in `Allowed-Origins` — so unattended-upgrades was never going
   to touch them. The blacklist gives up nothing that was previously automatic.

   To prove blacklist→skip end-to-end on a live pending package rather than trusting the mechanism,
   `curl` is upgradable from `noble-security/main`: dry-run, add a throwaway
   `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/53-blacklist-selftest` containing `"curl$"`, dry-run again and watch it
   vanish, then **delete the throwaway file**.
